    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "The main point of 'mini.bracketed' is [to created mappings using square brackets](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c122e852517adaf7257688e435369c050da113b1/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L17-L29). The specific targets [are documented](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c122e852517adaf7257688e435369c050da113b1/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L34-L65), which include `]d`, `]b`, `]l`, `]q`, and `]t`.\n\nThe first four do indeed have significant overlap, but 'mini.bracketed' functionality is indeed different in some areas. The most important one is how it works with `[count]`. For example, `2]B` will navigate to the second last buffer with 'mini.bracketed' mappings while it will navigate to buffer *number 2* with built-in mappings. I do believe that the behavior of 'mini.bracketed' is more intuitive as buffer numbers tend to be arbitrarily big numbers.\n\nTo not create particular mappings, [set `config.<target>.suffix = ''`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c122e852517adaf7257688e435369c050da113b1/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L135). For example, `require('mini.bracketed').setup({ treesitter = { suffix = '' } })`.",
            "created_at": "2025-07-28T18:35:01Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1914#issuecomment-3128483082",
